title A page that calls itself living is not testimony, whatever its filename says
prompt Autonomous lap under the baton -- Grass seat, the whole surface, auditing.
rota lap 4188, row 3 by git rev-list --count HEAD -- WATER, Venus, care and flow, sense of TASTE. The threshold page says taste only works up close: read the actual file, run the actual witness, sip the actual code. That sentence decided the lap. It also turned out to be a claim about the threshold page itself, which quoted a word its own dual seat had already dropped.
think Opened cold with --scoped and let it finish -- 159 guards, 1,501s, tree_moved=no, 4 red. Reds first.
think readme_reach and reds_fold share one root, as they did for the parked lap before me: reds_fold_witness.rish line 45 asserts reach.ok, so one broken living link reds two guards. The scan named it exactly -- construction/ITINERARY.md -> archive/REDS-a-label-is-not-its-target-rows-571.md, where the shelf on disk is rows-570.md. I read the shelf's own head to confirm it says %570 rather than trusting the filename. One character, two reds closed, broken_in_living 1 -> 0.
think The shelf whose link was wrong is the one whose subject is a label is not its target. A citation naming a target that is not the label, on the page teaching that distinction. Noted rather than dressed up.
think stash_record read unlanded=1: a Grass lap of 20260907.134135 parked whole by fleet_round_open.sh, its log carried by nothing. Landed verbatim, and then read its work file by file rather than assuming: the card link it fixed had been fixed by another hand and re-broken since; REDS-fold-recital.md carries its %566 line; glow_desk_reach_witness.rish was repaired upstream with a pwd|cksum per-ship suffix rather than that lap's mktemp pen, so that half is superseded; and its [_.] dated-spelling repair to two scans did NOT land and stands in stash@{0} still. Two obs lines appended at landing say so.
think Then the rota. I read the water threshold page whole, and its dual seat summarises growing-a-language as "stand on proven ground, wear a thin costume first, own the core by path, diverge by strengthening, dogfood and gate". growing-a-language.md line 32 reads "Live in it as first resident, and gate." The source was swept; the summary was not.
think dogfood is banned in every form by .claude/rules/vocabulary-first-resident.md, and a guard exists -- duty 1 of living_docs_lint.rish, rebuilt this morning by %540 onto tools/fixtures/l/living_prose_roster.sh. So I asked why the guard could not see it, rather than just fixing the word.
think The roster drops any path matching (^|/)[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{6}[_.], on the mark law's own test: a stamped basename IS testimony. That test was drawn to decide whether a PATH REFERENCE inside a file may be rewritten, and it is right there. Borrowed to decide whether a page's PROSE obeys the word bans, it answers a different question.
think Measured before proposing anything. 555 tracked pages fall to the stamp rule alone -- more than the 379 the roster reads. 147 of those carry a **Status:** line whose value is Living: 40 foundations pages on this very rota, 25 context/specs, 18 active-designing. Feeding just those 147 to the scan named 8 retired-word lines.
think Repaired by the page's own word rather than by a list: a stamped page re-enters when its **Status:** value is Living. The meter judges nothing, no roster is maintained, and a page that has declared itself living is exactly the page the vocabulary laws say to sweep on touch.
think Also exempted context/specs/20260707-053212_radiant-vocabulary-pass.md, which is the law seating footgun, dead-peer and sanity check -- four of the eight hits were that page naming the words it retires. The roster already exempts five such pages by name; this is the sixth, and it belongs with them rather than as an advisory nobody can act on.
obs THE CONTROL CAUGHT MY FIRST DRAFT, AND THAT IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. I matched /Living/ anywhere on the Status line. The control's new plant reads "**Status:** Retired -- a lifecycle word that is not Living", and it was named as a violation. A predicate that reads a line where it means to read a value -- which is %570's exact-against-loose lesson, met the same day, one instrument over, by the lap that had just repaired that shelf's own link. The fix reads **Status:** followed by Living as the value. Status stays unanchored on purpose: this tree writes "**Language:** EN - **Status:** Living, **mixed room**" as often as it writes Status first. Room is not read at all -- it answers the register question, never the living-or-testimony one.
obs THE ROSTER SIZE FELL WHEN THE PREDICATE GOT EXACT, 525 -> 520, and that fall is the measurement working. Five pages mention Living in a Status line that says something else.
obs NO GATE MOVED, AND I CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Duty 1 prints and refuses nothing -- living_docs_lint.rish says so in its own closing line and asserts only that the control passes. So widening its subject raises no ceiling and reds no tree, which is why this was a lap rather than a word. Had duty 1 been gated, the honest move would have been the number on the card and Keaton's word, not the change.
obs WHAT I DID NOT DO. Sweep the three remaining lines. They sit in living-declared context/specs pages, and a dated page's prose is Tier 2 -- style via a recorded Radiant pass -- so they turn on touch under the vocabulary rules rather than on an audit lap reaching across three subjects. One of them also carries a non-ASCII arrow, which is the same on-touch answer.
obs THIS IS %540'S NEXT LAYER RATHER THAN ITS CORRECTION. That row, landed eight hours before mine, moved duty 1 from a 60-path page roster to a 379-path prose roster and named the remainder as a decision. The decision was right for testimony and wrong for the 147 pages that had already answered the question in their own hand. Nothing of Copal's was removed; the exclusion still stands for every page that has not declared itself.
obs THE ROW'S SENSE FOUND THE FINDING, AGAIN. Taste works only up close, and a count would have read 379 pages and zero hits and called it green. Reading the actual page found a paraphrase quoting a word its own source had stopped saying -- on the page eight ships open every fifth lap.
