recommend check-in (Keaton) -- two questions the lap could not answer for itself. The card and the REDS pin both closed within 40 bytes of their bound, so a per-ship face is now all either can hold; and %524 stands where I left it -- readme_reach_scan reads 1,885 documents in under a second, which is cheap enough to stand at commit beside the shelf-row wall, yet moving a guard into tools/hooks/pre-commit changes what every ship pays per commit across eight trees.
obs THE SPINE COLLIDED AND CAUGHT ITSELF, WHICH IS THE LAW WORKING RATHER THAN FAILING. The rebase brought three upstream commits down, and construction/REDS.md conflicted on exactly one line -- the headline census, both sides reading 572, because upstream had booked %572 at 20260907.141734 while I booked mine at 145113. reds_spine_derive_witness reddened at once; my row is unshared, so it renumbers to %573 and the stamp stays its key. Then unshared_citation reddened because I had written %573 onto the card before xy binds it, which is derived-spine rule 4 biting the hand that had just cited it correctly everywhere else. The card cites the stamp.
obs THE PIN CAME BACK 2,106 BYTES OVER AND I FOLDED MY OWN ROW RATHER THAN A PEER'S. reds_pin_capacity read over_bound_foldable with 2 of 11 rows foldable; mine was BOOKED, its full account already stood on the landed-accounts shelf, and folding it touched nobody else's row. The card then stood 145 over from upstream's own growth, and the honest cut was my own block again rather than a peer's -- it closes at exactly 40,960, the bound to the byte, with e123_living_pin_guard GREEN.
